#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Flag confident NEGATIVE claims — the one sentence shape no other fleet check can see.

Every check we own verifies that a *stated* positive is still true: `check-blog-claims`
diffs field COUNTS and feature lists, `check-competitor-claims` diffs rival user counts,
`check-registry-fields` diffs output FIELDS. All of them read a claim that something IS
the case and ask the world whether it still is. **Nothing reads a claim that something is
NOT the case** — "there is no field that...", "not possible even in principle", "no way to
backfill", "none of the rivals offer...".

Why that gap cost us (cycle 2864, the incident this check exists for). The post
`google-play-developer-replied-event-only-fires-for-future-replies.md` shipped
2026-10-20 asserting "There's no field anywhere in the Play Store review payload that
says *when* a reply was posted ... the only timestamp the Actor ... can see is the
review's own `date`, not the reply's." Google Play ships `replyDate`, and
`google-play-reviews-scraper` had been emitting it on every output row since before the
post went up — it is in registry.json, the README sample output and the dataset field
list. The claim was wrong about OUR OWN PRODUCT, survived 18 days, and `check-blog-claims`
reported (and still reports) 0 stale claims on that post, because a field that is wrongly
said NOT to exist is never counted by a check that only looks at fields that ARE claimed.
It was caught by a human re-reading the post, not by any tool.

**The obvious mechanical rule does not work, and cycle 2980 measured that before
shipping one — do not re-propose it.** The rule queued at c2864 was: flag an absence
sentence that names a field the owning Actor's registry.json declares, and call that a
zero-false-positive contradiction. Measured over 123 documents it produced 16 hits of
which ~0 were real (nearly every one is a TRUE statement about the UPSTREAM payload that
happens to name a column we DERIVE — "There's no `currency` field on the product payload
itself", "a `parentCommentId` field that doesn't exist in the raw response"), and — the
decisive part — **it does not catch the sentence it was designed for.** The c2864 claim
was "There's no field anywhere in the Play Store review payload that says *when* a reply
was posted"; the field it was wrong about, `replyDate`, is not named in it at all. The
claim was about a CONCEPT, not a field name. Re-run of that sentence through this file's
own matchers: absence phrase True, declared-fields-named `[]`. So the rule misses it, and
fires only on the following sentence's correct, affirmative mention of `` `date` ``. A
check that cannot catch its founding incident must not be a gate.

What IS separable mechanically is **who the claim is about**, and that is the whole value
here. Two tiers:

  SELF-SCOPED (tier A — the c2864 class, small, every hit needs a human read)
      An absence phrase in the same sentence as a reference to our own product ("the
      Actor", "we", "our", the owning slug, a `/go/<slug>` or `/tools/<slug>` link). These
      are the claims we are ANSWERABLE for: the data to adjudicate them is in this repo
      (registry.json, input_schema.json, main.js), so a reader can settle each one without
      leaving the box. The c2864 sentence is in this set. Each hit prints the owning
      Actor's declared field list beside it so the adjudication is one glance, not a
      cross-reference by hand.

  UPSTREAM/RIVAL (tier B — counted, listed only with --all)
      Every other absence phrase. These are facts about openFDA, Greenhouse, a rival
      listing — no local data can adjudicate them and nothing in this repo should pretend
      to. Counted so the ratio stays visible, listed on request, never failed on.

Neither tier exits non-zero by default: an absence claim is not a defect, it is a
sentence that was never reviewed. `--strict` exits 1 if tier A is non-empty, for a
caller that wants the reading list enforced.

What is deliberately NOT flagged, and why:

  * blockquote lines (`>`-prefixed). A retracted claim is kept quoted in full on purpose
    (the c2864 correction does exactly this, under "Why the event can't fire
    retroactively"), so a quoted absence claim is a RECORD of a wrong claim, not a live
    one. Flagging it would permanently re-report the very incident this check was built
    from. Same reasoning as the LEARNINGS c2856 dated-record rule.
  * fenced code blocks and indented sample output — a comment inside a snippet is not
    customer-facing prose.
  * a bare lowercase field token. A field reference counts only if it is BACKTICKED or
    unambiguously camelCase, because 14 of the fleet's declared field names are ordinary
    English words (`date`, `title`, `price`, `free`, `text`, `description`, `summary`,
    `score`, `url`, `version`, `language`, `updated`, `released`, `installs`) and matching
    those bare would flag every sentence in the blog. The c2864 claim still trips the
    rule: it wrote `` `date` `` in backticks, which is the house style for a field.

Read-only. No network, no auth, no platform cost.

Usage:
  bin/check-absence-claims                   # tier A listed, tier B counted
  bin/check-absence-claims --all             # list tier B too
  bin/check-absence-claims --only <slug>     # restrict to one Actor's post(s)/README
  bin/check-absence-claims --fields <slug>   # print that Actor's declared field list
  bin/check-absence-claims --strict          # exit 1 if tier A is non-empty
"""
import glob
import json
import os
import re
import sys

ROOT = os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)))

# Sellable fields = registry entries minus the internal `_`-prefixed watch columns, the
# same definition check-blog-claims uses (its docstring note 1b, cycle 2300): the tool
# pages and READMEs never sell `_watchChangeType`/`_watchPrevious` as fields.
REG = {t["slug"]: [f for f in t.get("output_fields", []) if not f.startswith("_")]
       for t in json.load(open(os.path.join(ROOT, "actors/registry.json")))["tools"]}

# Confident-negative vocabulary. Each pattern is a claim that something does not or
# cannot exist, as opposed to a hedge ("may not", "we don't currently") -- a hedge is
# not the failure class (it invites a re-check by its own wording).
ABSENCE = [
    r"\bthere(?:'s| is| are| exists?)? no\b",
    r"\bno (?:\w+[- ]){0,3}(?:field|column|key|attribute|timestamp|parameter|endpoint|cursor|flag)s?\b",
    r"\bno way to\b",
    r"\bnot? possible\b",
    r"\beven in principle\b",
    r"\bnothing (?:in|on|about) the\b",
    r"\bnever (?:exposed?|returned?|emitted?|populated?|set|present)\b",
    r"\bnone of (?:the|our|its|them)\b",
    r"\bno such\b",
    r"\bdoes(?:n't| not) exist\b",
    r"\bis(?:n't| not) exposed\b",
    r"\bisn't anywhere\b",
    r"\bcannot be (?:recovered|backfilled|derived|reconstructed)\b",
    r"\bno (?:API|api) (?:for|that)\b",
    r"\bimpossible\b",
]
ABSENCE_RE = re.compile("|".join(ABSENCE), re.I)

# Shapes that match the absence vocabulary but are NOT an unreviewed capability claim.
# Each is exempt because another check already owns it, or because it is not a negative
# claim at all. Measured on the first full run (c2980): 126 + 3 hits, cutting the
# self-scoped reading list 185 -> 128 without dropping a capability claim. A third
# candidate ("nothing to violate" / "no ToS", the legal-posture line owned by
# check-disclosure) was written and then DELETED because it matched 0 sentences fleet-wide
# -- its one apparent hit was already caught by the no-proxy clause in the same sentence.
# Do not re-add a rule here without measuring its own hit count first.
EXEMPT = [
    # "No API key, no login, no proxy" is the fleet's standard POSITIVE selling line --
    # a statement about what the buyer does not have to supply, not a limit on us.
    (r"\bno (?:API |api )?key\b|\bno login\b|\bno proxy\b|\bno auth\b|\bno sign[- ]?up\b",
     "positive 'no key/login/proxy' selling line, not a capability limit"),
    # "there is no run size / no plan on which we are the cheaper choice" is a live price
    # comparison, owned by check-price-superiority + check-unit-matched-price.
    (r"\bno (?:run size|plan|tier|volume)\b.{0,60}\bcheaper\b|\bcheaper choice\b",
     "price comparison -- owned by check-price-superiority"),
]
EXEMPT_RE = [(re.compile(p, re.I), why) for p, why in EXEMPT]


def exempt(sentence):
    for rx, why in EXEMPT_RE:
        if rx.search(sentence):
            return why
    return None

# A field reference is a backticked token or an unambiguously camelCase bare word.
# Bare lowercase words are ignored on purpose -- see the docstring.
BACKTICKED = re.compile(r"`([A-Za-z_][\w.\[\]]*)`")
CAMEL = re.compile(r"\b([a-z]+[A-Z]\w*)\b")

SENTENCE_SPLIT = re.compile(r"(?<=[.!?])\s+(?=[A-Z`*\"])")


def front_matter(text):
    """The `key: value` block between the leading --- fences, or {}."""
    if not text.startswith("---"):
        return {}
    end = text.find("\n---", 3)
    if end < 0:
        return {}
    fm = {}
    for line in text[3:end].splitlines():
        if ":" in line and not line.startswith(" "):
            k, _, v = line.partition(":")
            fm[k.strip()] = v.strip().strip('"').strip("'")
    return fm


def prose_lines(text):
    """(lineno, line) for customer-facing prose only: no front matter, no fenced code,
    no `>` blockquote, no 4-space-indented sample output."""
    out, in_fence, in_fm = [], False, False
    for i, line in enumerate(text.splitlines(), 1):
        s = line.strip()
        if i == 1 and s == "---":
            in_fm = True
            continue
        if in_fm:
            if s == "---":
                in_fm = False
            continue
        if s.startswith("```") or s.startswith("~~~"):
            in_fence = not in_fence
            continue
        if in_fence or s.startswith(">") or line.startswith("    ") or not s:
            continue
        out.append((i, line))
    return out


# "this claim is about US". First-person and this-Actor references only -- a bare mention
# of the upstream vendor ("Greenhouse", "openFDA") is NOT self-scope, which is the whole
# point of the split. `/go/<slug>` is the click-logged CTA form every blog product link
# has used since cycle 1509; `/tools/<slug>` is the on-site tool page.
SELF = re.compile(
    r"\b(?:we|we're|we've|our|ours|us|this Actor|the Actor|our Actor|this scraper|"
    r"fetchsmith)\b|/go/|/tools/", re.I)


def self_scoped(sentence, owner):
    """True if the sentence refers to our own product. Matching the owning slug or its
    words counts too (`google-play-reviews-scraper`, 'this Actor', '[ats-jobs-scraper]')."""
    if SELF.search(sentence):
        return True
    return bool(owner) and owner in sentence


def field_refs(sentence, fields):
    """Declared fields of `fields` that this sentence references in a form precise enough
    to be a field reference (backticked, or camelCase)."""
    seen = set(BACKTICKED.findall(sentence)) | set(CAMEL.findall(sentence))
    # a backticked `obj.replyDate` or `row['replyDate']` still names the field
    for tok in list(seen):
        for part in re.split(r"[.\[\]'\"]+", tok):
            if part:
                seen.add(part)
    return [f for f in fields if f in seen]


def scan(path, owner):
    """Absence-phrased sentences in one document. owner = slug this doc is about, or None."""
    text = open(path, encoding="utf-8").read()
    hits = []
    for lineno, line in prose_lines(text):
        for sent in SENTENCE_SPLIT.split(line):
            if not ABSENCE_RE.search(sent):
                continue
            sent = sent.strip()
            if exempt(sent):
                continue
            own = field_refs(sent, REG.get(owner, [])) if owner else []
            hits.append((lineno, sent, own, self_scoped(sent, owner)))
    return hits


def main(argv):
    only, show_all, strict = None, False, False
    args = argv[1:]
    while args:
        a = args.pop(0)
        if a == "--only":
            only = args.pop(0)
        elif a == "--all":
            show_all = True
        elif a == "--strict":
            strict = True
        elif a == "--fields":
            slug = args.pop(0)
            if slug not in REG:
                sys.exit("unknown slug %r -- not in registry.json" % slug)
            print("%s declares %d sellable field(s):" % (slug, len(REG[slug])))
            print("  " + ", ".join(REG[slug]))
            return 0
        else:
            sys.exit(__doc__)

    docs = []  # (path, owner_slug_or_None, label)
    for p in sorted(glob.glob(os.path.join(ROOT, "site/content/blog/*.md"))):
        tool = front_matter(open(p, encoding="utf-8").read()).get("tool") or None
        docs.append((p, tool if tool in REG else None, "blog"))
    for p in sorted(glob.glob(os.path.join(ROOT, "actors/*/README.md"))):
        slug = os.path.basename(os.path.dirname(p))
        docs.append((p, slug if slug in REG else None, "readme"))

    selfscoped, upstream = [], []
    scanned = 0
    for path, owner, label in docs:
        if only and owner != only:
            continue
        scanned += 1
        rel = os.path.relpath(path, ROOT)
        for lineno, sent, own, mine in scan(path, owner):
            (selfscoped if mine else upstream).append((rel, lineno, label, owner, own, sent))

    print("SELF-SCOPED (%d) -- absence claims about OUR OWN product. Adjudicate each from"
          " this repo (registry.json / input_schema.json / main.js); an absence claim is"
          " not a defect, it is a sentence nothing has ever re-read." % len(selfscoped))
    lastdoc = None
    for rel, lineno, label, owner, own, sent in selfscoped:
        if rel != lastdoc:
            lastdoc = rel
            if owner:
                print("\n  %s  [%s, %d declared field(s)]" % (rel, owner, len(REG[owner])))
                print("      fields: %s" % ", ".join(REG[owner]))
            else:
                print("\n  %s  [no owning Actor]" % rel)
        print("    :%d  %s" % (lineno, sent[:400]))
        if own:
            print("         ^ names own declared field(s): %s" % ", ".join(own))

    if show_all:
        print("\nUPSTREAM/RIVAL (%d) -- about openFDA/Greenhouse/a rival listing; no local"
              " data can settle these:" % len(upstream))
        for rel, lineno, label, owner, own, sent in upstream:
            print("  %s:%d  %s" % (rel, lineno, sent[:200]))
    else:
        print("\nUPSTREAM/RIVAL: %d (not about us -- pass --all to list)." % len(upstream))

    print("\n%d document(s) scanned, %d self-scoped, %d upstream/rival."
          % (scanned, len(selfscoped), len(upstream)))
    return 1 if (strict and selfscoped) else 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv))
